        "explanation": "Shahada means 'Declaration of Faith' — it's the statement that makes someone Muslim."
      },
      {
        "q": "How many parts does the Shahada have?",
        "options": [
          "One part",
          "Two parts",
          "Three parts"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "The Shahada has two parts: believing in one God and believing Muhammad is His messenger."
      },
      {
        "q": "Who is the last prophet mentioned in the Shahada?",
        "options": [
          "Prophet Musa",
          "Prophet Isa",
          "Prophet Muhammad"
        ],
        "correct": 2,
        "explanation": "The Shahada mentions Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as Allah's final messenger."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "salah",
    "title": "Salah - Prayer",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/imgi_11_topic_prayer_salah-AiQHjTprGERKrV6ZWGWS6N.webp?updatedAt=1781117901214",
    "emoji": "🤲",
    "category": "pillars_of_islam",
    "content": {
      "starter": "Salah is talking to Allah! Five times a day, we stop what we're doing, wash our hands and face (that's called wudu), face Mecca, and pray. It's like a special phone call to Allah. He loves when we talk to Him!",
      "explorer": "Salah is the second pillar of Islam. Muslims pray five times a day: Fajr (dawn), Dhuhr (noon), Asr (afternoon), Maghrib (sunset), and Isha (night). Each prayer involves standing, bowing, and prostrating while reciting verses from the Quran. Salah keeps us connected to Allah throughout the day and teaches us discipline and gratitude.",
      "thinker": "The five daily prayers (Salawat) are a gift from Allah, prescribed during the Night Journey (Isra and Mi'raj). Each prayer corresponds to a spiritual state: Fajr purifies the soul before the day begins, Dhuhr provides a midday recharge, Asr encourages reflection, Maghrib marks gratitude for the day's blessings, and Isha offers peace before sleep. The physical movements — standing (qiyam), bowing (ruku), and prostration (sujud) — are acts of humility that remind us of our relationship with our Creator."
    },
    "funFact": "Did you know? Muslims face the Kaaba in Mecca when they pray, no matter where they are in the world! Even from space, astronauts pray toward Mecca!",
    "quiz": [
      {
        "q": "How many times a day do Muslims pray?",
        "options": [
          "Three times",
          "Five times",
          "Seven times"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "Muslims pray five times a day: Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha."
      },
      {
        "q": "What do we do before praying?",
        "options": [
          "Eat food",
          "Perform wudu (ablution)",
          "Read a book"
        ],
