  {
    "id": "hajj",
    "title": "Hajj - Pilgrimage to Mecca",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/hajj_overview-9BS5YUf8qgFKvNgAVo2DpT.webp?updatedAt=1781117901446",
    "emoji": "🕋",
    "category": "pillars_of_islam",
    "content": {
      "starter": "Hajj is a big trip to Mecca! Every Muslim who is able goes there to pray around the Kaaba — Allah's house. Millions of people go together, wearing simple white clothes. Everyone is equal there — rich or poor, it doesn't matter!",
      "explorer": "Hajj is the fifth pillar of Islam — a pilgrimage to Mecca that every Muslim must make at least once in their lifetime if they are able. It happens during the Islamic month of Dhul Hijjah. Pilgrims wear simple white clothes called Ihram, circling the Kaaba, walking between hills, and standing in prayer at Arafat. Hajj teaches equality, patience, and devotion to Allah.",
      "thinker": "Hajj is the world's largest annual gathering and represents the ultimate expression of Islamic unity. Following the footsteps of Prophet Ibrahim ﷺ and his family, pilgrims perform rituals that symbolize sacrifice, purification, and submission to Allah. The wearing of Ihram strips away all markers of wealth and status — everyone stands equal before Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Hajj is an obligation once in a lifetime for whoever has the ability.' It is a profound spiritual journey that cleanses sins and renews one's connection with the Creator."
    },
    "funFact": "Did you know? Over 2 million people perform Hajj every year! When pilgrims throw stones at the pillars (Jamarat), they're following the example of Prophet Ibrahim!",
