  {
    "id": "how_to_pray",
    "title": "How to Pray: A Complete Guide",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/imgi_11_topic_prayer_salah-AiQHjTprGERKrV6ZWGWS6N.webp?updatedAt=1781117901214",
    "emoji": "🕌",
    "category": "daily_practices",
    "content": {
      "starter": "How do Muslims pray? It starts with Wudu — washing your hands, face, arms, and feet to get clean for Allah. Then you face the Kaaba in Mecca and do special movements: standing, bowing, and putting your forehead on the ground. Each prayer has a name like Fajr (morning) and Maghrib (sunset). There are 5 prayers every day! It's like having 5 special meetings with Allah.\n\nWUDU — The 8 Steps:\n1) Niyyah — Make the intention in your heart to perform Wudu for the sake of Allah. 2) Wash Hands — Wash both hands up to the wrists three times, starting with the right hand. Make sure water reaches between your fingers! 3) Rinse Mouth — Take water into your mouth and rinse it thoroughly three times. 4) Rinse Nose — Breathe water in through your nose and blow it out three times. 5) Wash Face — Wash your entire face three times, from forehead to chin and ear to ear. 6) Wash Arms — Wash both arms up to the elbows three times, starting with the right arm. 7) Wipe Head — Wipe your entire head once with wet hands. 8) Wash Feet — Wash both feet up to the ankles three times, starting with the right foot. Make sure water reaches between your toes!\n\nRemember: Take your time with each step, use clean water, and avoid waste. Wudu brings purity to your body and light to your heart!\n\nSALAH — The 7 Main Positions:\n1) Niyyah — Intend in your heart to pray. 2) Takbir — Raise your hands to your ears and say 'Allahu Akbar' (Allah is the Greatest). 3) Qiyam — Stand upright with hands crossed at chest level, and recite Surah Al-Fatiha and another Surah. 4) Ruku — Bow forward with your hands on your knees, and say 'Subhana Rabbiyal Azeem' (Glory be to my Lord, the Greatest). 5) Qawmah — Rise from bowing and say 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah' (Allah hears those who praise Him). 6) Sujud — Put your forehead, nose, hands, knees, and toes on the ground, and say 'Subhana Rabbiyal A'la' (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High). 7) Tashahhud — Sit and recite the testimony of faith and send blessings upon Prophet Muhammad.\n\nTHE 5 DAILY PRAYERS:\n- Fajr (Dawn): 2 Fard + 2 Sunnah = 4 rak'ahs — strengthens your faith!\n- Dhuhr (Noon): 4 Fard + 6 Sunnah = 10 rak'ahs — find peace and clarity!\n- Asr (Afternoon): 4 Fard + 4 Sunnah = 8 rak'ahs — seek Allah's pleasure!\n- Maghrib (Sunset): 3 Fard + 2 Sunnah = 5 rak'ahs — build discipline and gratitude!\n- Isha (Night): 4 Fard + 2 Sunnah = 6 rak'ahs — connect with your Creator!\nTotal daily: 17 Fard + 12 Sunnah = 29 rak'ahs combined.",
      "explorer": "How to Pray: A Complete Guide covers Wudu (ablution), Salah (prayer), the 5 daily prayers, and the rak'ah breakdown in full detail.\n\nWUDU — The 8 Steps of Ablution:\nWudu is the ritual purification performed before prayer. It is a physical and spiritual cleansing that prepares the body and mind for communication with Allah. Wudu is obligatory before performing Salah and is considered one of the most important prerequisites for prayer. The steps are: 1) Niyyah — make the intention in your heart to perform Wudu for prayer, 2) Wash both hands up to the wrists three times starting with the right hand, ensuring water reaches between fingers, 3) Rinse your mouth thoroughly three times — this cleanses the mouth for recitation, 4) Breathe water in through the nose and blow it out three times using your left hand to assist, 5) Wash your entire face three times from forehead to chin and ear to ear, including the beard if you have one, 6) Wash both arms up to and including the elbows three times starting with the right arm, 7) Wipe your head once with wet hands — you can wipe the entire head or just a portion, 8) Wash both feet up to and including the ankles three times starting with the right foot, ensuring water reaches between the toes.\n\nWhen is Wudu Required? Before every obligatory prayer (Fard), before voluntary prayers (Sunnah/Nafl), when touching the Quran, and after using the bathroom.\n\nSALAH — The 7 Main Positions of Prayer:\nSalah is the Islamic prayer performed five times daily at prescribed times. It is the second pillar of Islam and a direct connection between the believer and Allah. Each prayer consists of one or more rak'ahs (cycles). The seven positions are: 1) Niyyah (Intention) — intend in your heart to pray the specific prayer (Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, or Isha). The intention must be clear and sincere. 2) Takbir (Opening) — raise your hands to your ears and say 'Allahu Akbar' (Allah is the Greatest). This marks the beginning of your prayer. 3) Qiyam (Standing) — stand upright with your hands crossed at chest level. Recite Surah Al-Fatiha and another Surah from the Quran. 4) Ruku (Bowing) — bow forward with your hands on your knees, keeping your back parallel to the ground. Say 'Subhana Rabbiyal Azeem' (Glory be to my Lord, the Greatest). 5) Qawmah (Rising) — rise from bowing to the standing position. Say 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah' (Allah hears those who praise Him). 6) Sujud (Prostration) — prostrate with your forehead, nose, hands, knees, and toes touching the ground. Say 'Subhana Rabbiyal A'la' (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High). 7) Tashahhud (Testimony) — sit between prostrations or at the end of prayer. Recite the testimony of faith and send blessings upon Prophet Muhammad.\n\nImportant Notes About Salah: Always face the Qibla (direction of the Kaaba in Mecca), maintain proper body covering (Satr): men from navel to below knees, women entire body except hands and face, perform Wudu before every prayer, pray at the prescribed times, and concentrate and be present during prayer (Khushu) for spiritual benefit.\n\nTHE 5 DAILY PRAYERS:\n- Fajr (Dawn): Before sunrise. 2 Fard + 2 Sunnah = 4 total rak'ahs. The dawn prayer marks the beginning of the Islamic day and strengthens your faith.\n- Dhuhr (Noon): After the sun passes its zenith. 4 Fard + 4 Sunnah Muakkadah + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah = 10 total rak'ahs. The longest prayer of the day helps find peace and clarity.\n- Asr (Afternoon): Late afternoon before sunset. 4 Fard + 4 Sunnah Muakkadah + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah = 8 total rak'ahs. Seek Allah's pleasure in the late afternoon.\n- Maghrib (Sunset): Just after the sun sets. 3 Fard + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah = 5 total rak'ahs. Unique among the five prayers with 3 Fard rak'ahs. Builds discipline and gratitude.\n- Isha (Night): After nightfall. 4 Fard + 2 Sunnah Muakkadah + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah = 8 total rak'ahs. Connect with your Creator before sleep.\n\nTotal Daily: 17 Fard rak'ahs + 12 Sunnah Muakkadah = 29 combined total.",
      "thinker": "How to Pray: A Complete Guide provides comprehensive instruction on Wudu (ablution), Salah (prayer), the five daily prayers, and the complete rak'ah breakdown.\n\nWUDU (Ablution):\nWudu is the ritual purification that serves as both a physical cleansing and spiritual preparation for Salah. It is obligatory before every Fard (obligatory) prayer, Sunnah/Nafl (voluntary) prayers, and before touching the Quran. The eight steps follow a specific sequence established by the Sunnah of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ: 1) Niyyah — the internal intention, 2) Washing hands up to the wrists three times, 3) Rinsing the mouth thoroughly three times, 4) Inhaling water through the nose and expelling it three times, 5) Washing the entire face three times (from hairline to chin and ear to ear, including the beard for men), 6) Washing both arms up to and including the elbows three times, 7) Wiping the head once with wet hands, 8) Washing both feet up to and including the ankles three times. Each step has precise requirements: water must reach between fingers and toes, and all parts must be washed in the correct order. Wudu is invalidated by natural discharges, deep sleep, loss of consciousness, and touching one's private parts.\n\nSALAH (Prayer) — The Seven Positions:\n1) Niyyah (النية): The internal intention specifying which prayer is being performed. It must be clear and sincere. 2) Takbir (التكبير): The opening 'Allahu Akbar' when raising hands to the ears, marking the sacred boundary of prayer. 3) Qiyam (القيام): Standing position with hands folded at chest level (right hand over left), reciting Al-Fatiha followed by a second Surah. 4) Ruku (الركوع): Bowing with hands on knees, back parallel to ground, reciting 'Subhana Rabbiyal Azeem' (Glory be to my Lord, the Greatest) three times. 5) Qawmah (القومة): Rising from Ruku to standing with 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah, Rabbana lakal hamd' (Allah hears those who praise Him, our Lord, all praise is for You). 6) Sujud (السجود): Prostration with seven points of contact — forehead, nose, both hands, both knees, and toes — reciting 'Subhana Rabbiyal A'la' (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High) three times. Performed twice in each rak'ah. 7) Tashahhud (التشهد): Sitting position for the testimony of faith (At-Tahiyyatu lillahi was-salawatu wat-tayyibat), the Salawat (Allahumma salli 'ala Muhammad), and the Du'a.\n\nTHE FIVE DAILY PRAYERS — Complete Breakdown:\n- Fajr: 2 Fard + 2 Sunnah Muakkadah (before Fard) = 4 rak'ahs (before sunrise, approximately 4:30-5:30 AM)\n- Dhuhr: 4 Sunnah Muakkadah (before Fard) + 4 Fard + 2 Sunnah Muakkadah (after Fard) + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah (after) = 10-12 rak'ahs (midday, approximately 12:00-1:00 PM)\n- Asr: 4 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah (before Fard) + 4 Fard = 4-8 rak'ahs (late afternoon, approximately 3:30-4:30 PM)\n- Maghrib: 3 Fard + 2 Sunnah Muakkadah (after Fard) + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah (after) = 5-7 rak'ahs (after sunset, approximately 6:45-7:15 PM)\n- Isha: 4 Fard + 2 Sunnah Muakkadah (after Fard) + 2 Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah (after) = 6-8 rak'ahs (after nightfall, approximately 8:15-9:30 PM)\n\nRAK'AH BREAKDOWN TABLE:\n| Prayer | Fard | Sunnah Muakkadah | Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah | Total |\n| Fajr | 2 | 2 | 0 | 4 |\n| Dhuhr | 4 | 4 | 2 | 10 |\n| Asr | 4 | 0 | 2 | 6 |\n| Maghrib | 3 | 0 | 2 | 5 |\n| Isha | 4 | 2 | 2 | 8 |\n| TOTAL | 17 | 8 | 8 | 33 |\n\nTypes of Prayers: Fard (Obligatory) — compulsory, missing them is a grave sin. Sunnah Muakkadah (Strongly Recommended) — highly emphasized, the Prophet ﷺ regularly performed them. Sunnah Ghair Muakkadah (Optional) — the Prophet ﷺ occasionally performed them, bringing extra reward.\n\nPrayer is not merely physical movement — it demands Khushu (concentration and humility) and sincere connection with Allah. As Allah says: 'Indeed, prayer prohibits immorality and wrongdoing' (Quran 29:45)."
    },
    "funFact": "Did you know? The five daily prayers were prescribed during Prophet Muhammad's Night Journey (Isra and Mi'raj) to heaven. Originally, Allah commanded 50 prayers per day, but after several reductions through Prophet Musa's advice, it became 5 — yet the reward remains equal to 50!",
    "quiz": [
      {
        "q": "How many steps are there in Wudu?",
        "options": [
          "6 steps",
