    "content": {
      "starter": "Eid al-Fitr is the happy day after Ramadan! We wear new clothes, eat yummy food, give gifts, and say 'Eid Mubarak!' to everyone. We give money to poor people called Zakat al-Fitr. It's like a big party for Allah!",
      "explorer": "Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan and is one of the two major Islamic holidays. Muslims gather for a special prayer, give Zakat al-Fitr (charity to the poor), wear their best clothes, and celebrate with family and friends. Children receive gifts and money (Eidi). It's a day of joy, gratitude, and community after a month of fasting and worship.",
      "thinker": "Eid al-Fitr ('Festival of Breaking the Fast') is celebrated on the first day of Shawwal, the month following Ramadan. It begins with a special congregational prayer and sermon, followed by Zakat al-Fitr — a mandatory charity given before the Eid prayer to ensure even the poorest can celebrate. The Prophet ﷺ would wear his best clothes on Eid and apply perfume. Eid is a day of celebration, but also of reflection — thanking Allah for the strength to complete Ramadan and asking for acceptance of our worship. It strengthens community bonds as Muslims visit relatives, share meals, and reconcile differences."
    },
