  {
    "id": "tawheed",
    "title": "Tawheed - Oneness of God",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/imgi_21_topic_abrahamic_religions-24Ub5MbtndBYdeZHxSMfyV.webp?updatedAt=1781117902606",
    "emoji": "☝️",
    "category": "core_beliefs",
    "content": {
      "starter": "Tawheed means there is ONLY ONE God — Allah! He created everything: you, me, the stars, the animals, everything! We only pray to Allah, not to anyone else. Allah is the only one who deserves our love and worship!",
      "explorer": "Tawheed is the most important belief in Islam — that there is only one God, Allah. He has no partners, no family, and no equals. He is the Creator of everything and the only one worthy of worship. Understanding Tawheed means we don't pray to statues, people, or anything else — only to Allah. This belief is the foundation of everything in Islam.",
      "thinker": "Tawheed is the cornerstone of Islamic theology, encompassing three dimensions: Tawheed ar-Rububiyyah (Oneness of Lordship), Tawheed al-Uluhiyyah (Oneness of Worship), and Tawheed al-Asma wa'l-Sifat (Oneness of Names and Attributes). The first recognizes Allah as the sole Creator and Sustainer. The second directs all forms of worship exclusively to Him. The third affirms that Allah's attributes are unique and incomparable. The Quran states: 'Say, He is Allah, the One. Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is begotten. And there is none comparable to Him' (Surah Al-Ikhlas 112:1-4)."
    },
    "funFact": "Did you know? The word 'Islam' comes from the root 'salaam' which means peace, and it means submission to Allah — the One God!",
    "quiz": [
      {
        "q": "What does Tawheed mean?",
        "options": [
          "Many gods",
          "Oneness of God",
          "Prayer"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "Tawheed means the Oneness of God — believing there is only one God, Allah."
      },
      {
        "q": "How many gods does Islam teach us about?",
        "options": [
          "Many gods",
          "Only one God",
          "Two gods"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "Islam teaches us that there is only one God — Allah."
      },
      {
        "q": "Which surah of the Quran talks about Tawheed?",
        "options": [
          "Al-Fatiha",
          "Al-Ikhlas",
          "Al-Baqarah"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "Surah Al-Ikhlas (112) talks about the Oneness of God."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "angels",
    "title": "Angels",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/imgi_21_topic_abrahamic_religions-24Ub5MbtndBYdeZHxSMfyV.webp?updatedAt=1781117902606",
    "emoji": "👼",
    "category": "core_beliefs",
    "content": {
      "starter": "Angels are special creatures made of light! Allah sends them to help us. Some angels write down our good and bad deeds. Some angels take our souls when it's time. Angels always obey Allah and never make mistakes!",
      "explorer": "Angels are one of the six articles of faith in Islam. They are created from light and always obey Allah. The most famous angel is Jibril (Gabriel), who brought Allah's messages to the prophets. Other important angels include Mikail (Michael), Israfil (who will blow the trumpet on Judgment Day), and Azra'il (the angel of death). Angels record our deeds and protect us.",
      "thinker": "Angels (Mala'ika) are a fundamental article of Islamic belief. Created from light, they exist in a different dimension and serve Allah in various capacities. Jibril (Gabriel) delivered the Quran to Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The Kiraman Katibin are recording angels who document every deed. The angel Munkar and Nakir question the dead in their graves. Unlike humans, angels have no free will — they always obey Allah. Their existence reminds us that we are never truly alone and that our actions are always being witnessed."
