    "content": {
      "starter": "Halal means 'okay to eat' in Arabic! Allah tells us what food is good for us. We can eat fruits, vegetables, chicken, and fish. We can't eat pork or drink alcohol. We always say 'Bismillah' before eating to thank Allah!",
      "explorer": "Halal means permissible in Islam. Allah has told us which foods are halal (allowed) and haram (forbidden). Halal foods include fruits, vegetables, grains, and properly slaughtered meat. Haram foods include pork, blood, alcohol, and meat not slaughtered in Allah's name. Saying 'Bismillah' before eating is a Sunnah and reminds us that food is a blessing from Allah.",
      "thinker": "The concept of halal and haram in food is rooted in the Quran and Sunnah. Allah says: 'Eat of what is lawful and good on the earth' (Surah Al-Baqarah 2:168). Halal is not just about what we eat but how food is obtained — it must be through ethical, humane, and clean methods. Pork is prohibited (haram) as stated in multiple Quranic verses. Alcohol is forbidden because it clouds the mind and leads to sin. Even when eating halal food, Islam teaches moderation: 'Eat and drink, but do not be excessive' (Surah Al-A'raf 31). The act of saying 'Bismillah' before eating transforms a routine meal into an act of worship."
