  {
    "id": "eid_al_adha",
    "title": "Eid al-Adha",
    "image": "https://ik.imagekit.io/4zbzbdytp/imgi_43_eid_adha_celebration-a8nckcQaGvn3CYJJy7ta5A.webp?updatedAt=1781117903366",
    "emoji": "🐑",
    "category": "special_times",
    "content": {
      "starter": "Eid al-Adha is the big Eid! It remembers when Prophet Ibrahim was ready to sacrifice his son for Allah. Allah was so happy with his faith that He sent a sheep instead! We sacrifice an animal and share the meat with family and poor people!",
      "explorer": "Eid al-Adha ('Festival of Sacrifice') honors Prophet Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son Ismail for Allah. Before Eid, Muslims who can afford it sacrifice an animal (sheep, goat, cow, or camel) and divide the meat into three parts: one for family, one for friends and neighbors, one for the poor. It coincides with the Hajj pilgrimage and is one of the two major Islamic holidays.",
      "thinker": "Eid al-Adha commemorates one of the most profound acts of faith in Islamic history — Prophet Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son Ismail in complete obedience to Allah. Just as Ibrahim was about to carry out the sacrifice, Allah replaced Ismail with a ram, and the sacrifice was accepted symbolically. This event is commemorated by millions of Muslims worldwide who sacrifice an animal during the days of Eid. The meat is divided into thirds: family, friends/neighbors, and the poor. Eid al-Adha coincides with the Hajj pilgrimage — while pilgrims are at Arafat, Muslims worldwide celebrate this festival of sacrifice, gratitude, and community."
    },
    "funFact": "Did you know? Eid al-Adha remembers when Prophet Ibrahim was ready to sacrifice his son for Allah — but Allah sent a ram instead as a test of faith!",
    "quiz": [
